Title:
IOCTL Parser

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
IOCTL Parser is a small GUI tool for decoding Windows I/O control codes (IOCTLs) into their constituent fields (device type, function, method, access). It helps reverse engineers and driver/security researchers quickly identify what an IOCTL value represents without manually doing bit math or looking up constants. The tool includes an embedded offline mapping sourced from the Windows IOCTL Reference (ioctls.net) to resolve device/function names, and it supports extending these mappings via user-supplied INI configuration files. It’s useful when analyzing kernel drivers, user↔kernel communication surfaces, and potential vulnerable IOCTL handlers. The project is implemented in C++ with Qt (6.5 LTS) and built via CMake, with optional packaging via CPack.

Technical Focus:
- Windows IOCTL encoding/decoding (CTL_CODE bitfields)
- DeviceIoControl interface and driver control codes
- Offline IOCTL name resolution (device/function databases)
- Extensible configuration via INI files
- Qt-based GUI tooling for reverse engineering workflows

Use Cases:
- Rapidly decode IOCTL constants found in drivers, binaries, or telemetry
- Triage driver attack surface by enumerating/understanding exposed IOCTLs
- Assist exploit development and fuzzing setup by clarifying method/access/function values
- Build/extend internal IOCTL dictionaries for proprietary drivers via custom INI mappings

Keywords:
IOCTL, CTL_CODE, DeviceIoControl, Windows kernel drivers, METHOD_BUFFERED, METHOD_IN_DIRECT, METHOD_OUT_DIRECT, METHOD_NEITHER, FILE_ANY_ACCESS, FILE_READ_ACCESS, FILE_WRITE_ACCESS, device type, function code, access mask, ioctls.net, Windows IOCTL Reference, Qt 6.5, CMake, CPack, reverse engineering, driver security